          People buy one of these expecting it to do the other's job. A non-contact voltage tester will not tell you which breaker feeds an outlet, and a breaker finder will not tell you a wire is safe to touch. Used together, they make a simple, safe routine; used alone, each leaves a gap that matters.
        </p>

        <TopPick />

        <div id="short" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. The Short Answer
          </h2>

          <p>
            A circuit breaker finder answers 'which breaker controls this outlet or light?'. You plug its transmitter into the circuit and sweep its receiver along the panel. A non-contact voltage tester, often called a volt pen or voltage sniffer, answers 'is there voltage here right now?'. You hold its tip near an outlet slot, a cord or a wire and it beeps or lights if it senses alternating voltage.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you are about to work on an outlet, switch or fixture, you need both answers. The finder gets you to the right breaker quickly; the tester confirms that switching it off actually made the thing you are about to touch dead. Neither question substitutes for the other.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you can only buy one, buy the voltage tester. It costs less, it covers the safety question, and you can find breakers without a finder by elimination. The finder is the time-saver; the tester is the safety check. Many electricians would add a third tool, a two-pole contact tester or multimeter, for confirming at bare conductors.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
